PURPOSE. This page determines the latitude of the Göttingen observatory and the index error of the Reichenbach meridian circle. It uses meridian zenith distances of α Aquilae (Altair) taken in two circle positions in Nov 1818 – Jan 1819. It is the twin of the α Arietis page before it (latitude 51°31'48.68") and the γ² Ursae minoris page after it (index error 7'46"03). Header: α = 295°29'0" (19h41m56s) and δ ≈ +8°23'52", the approximate place used for the star constants.

DATA TABLE, series I (circle position 1). Columns are:
- date;
- circle reading (43°14'48"69 and so on);
- barometer in Paris inches and lines (27" + lines);
- attached (inner) thermometer and outer thermometer, °Réaumur;
- day numbers in the form later standard as Bessel's A, B, C, D.

A is given as τ plus a nutation part, e.g. 0.8254 − 0.1601. B is given as two parts, −7.83 − 0.03. C and D are the aberration day numbers (+13.90, +13.97).

Plausibility check (mine): on Nov 5 the Sun's longitude is ≈223°. Then C = −20.25 cos ε cos ⊙ ≈ +13.6 and D = −20.25 sin ⊙ ≈ +13.8, matching the listed values.

Means of series I (6 nights):
- reading 43°14'50.99";
- barometer 27"8.33 lines;
- thermometers +3.37 and −0.23;
- A = mean of (τ + nutation part) = 0.82182;
- B = mean of the two-part sums = −7.71;
- C = 0.297, D = 18.732.

Means of series II (other circle position):
- reading 42°59'13.65";
- barometer 27"7.97;
- thermometers +5.0 and +0.85;
- A = 0.74445, B = −7.632, C = 7.255, D = 18.833.

REFRACTION (Bessel-type formula, log r = log α(z) + log tan z + barometer term + thermometer term).
- log α = 1.75970 (α ≈ 57.5").
- log tan 43°7' = 9.97144.
- Sum 1.73114, i.e. 53.84".
- Barometer term, with the barometer reduced for its own (inner) temperature: −78 for I and −141 for II, in units of 10^-5.
- Air-temperature term: +1590 for I and +1363 for II. The difference of 227 units for about 1.1°R matches the air expansion of ≈1/213 per °R.
- Results: log r = 1.74626, r = 55.75" (I); log r = 1.74336, r = 55.38" (II).

REDUCTION TO MEAN PLACE 1819.0 (my reading). Δδ = A·a' + B·b' + C·c' + D·d', with A taken relative to 1819.0, i.e. A − 1. The logged star constants are:
- [1] log n = 1.30198 (precession in declination, n = 20.04"/yr);
- [2] log cos α = 9.63372;
- [3] log sin δ = 9.16448;
- [4] log sin α = 9.95555n;
- [5] log cos δ = 9.99532;
- [6] log tan ε = 9.63756 (ε ≈ 23°28').

From these:
- a' = n cos α, log 0.93570 (8.624);
- b' = −sin α, log 9.95555 (0.90274);
- c' = tan ε cos δ − sin α sin δ. Gauss uses an addition (Gaussian) logarithm: log tan ε cos δ = 9.63288, log(−sin α sin δ) = 9.12003, addition log 0.11628, giving log c' = 9.74916 (c' = 0.56125);
- d' = cos α sin δ, log 8.79820 (0.06284).

Logs of the day numbers:
- series I: log(A−1) = log(−0.17818) = 9.25086n; log 7.71 = 0.88705n; log 0.297 = 9.47276; log 18.732 = 1.27259;
- series II: log(−0.25555) = 9.40747n; log 7.632 = 0.88264n; log 7.255 = 0.86064; log 18.833 = 1.27492.

Terms:
- series I: −1.537, −6.960, +0.167, +1.177. Subtotals (A+B) = −8.497 and (C+D) = +1.344, so Δδ_I = −7.153.
- series II: −2.204, −6.889, +4.072, +1.183. Subtotals −9.093 and +5.255, so Δδ_II = −3.838.

REDUCED ZENITH DISTANCES.
- I: 43°14'50.99" + 55.75" = 43°15'46.74"; then −7.15" gives 43°15'39.59".
- II: 42°59'13.65" + 55.38" = 43°0'9.03"; then −3.84" gives 43°0'5.19".
- Half difference = index error = 7'47.20".
- Means: raw 43°7'2.32"; refracted 43°7'57.88"; reduced z = 43°7'52.39".

LATITUDE. 51°31'50" is the assumed latitude used above. With Pond's north polar distance 81°36'4.44", δ = 8°23'55.56". Since the star culminates south of the zenith, φ = z + δ = 43°7'52.39" + 8°23'55.56" = 51°31'47.95".

AUXILIARY ENTRIES.
- 'Nov. 2860.07, Dec. 2890.07, Jan. 2922.07' are day counts from 1811 Jan 0 to Nov 0, Dec 0 and Jan 0 (2922 = 8 × 365.25). They presumably served as arguments for tables used in the day numbers.
- The little sums −0.48379 − 1 + 0.8208 = −0.663 and −0.72605 − 1 + 0.8208 = −0.905 are arithmetically right, but their purpose is unclear to me. −0.48379 also heads the α Arietis page.
